issue.body:
Hi.

I am working with multiple vehicles. [In the docs](https://sdk.dronecode.org/en/guide/connections.html), it's mentioned how to access a system by
```c++
DronecodeSDK dc;
//... 
//Use UUID named uuid to get the associated System
System &system = dc.system(uuid);
```

What is a correct way to create a variable number of multiple systems?

Thanks.

comments.body:
@julianoes so, I am looking into how to get `system_id` instead of uuid. I found this: https://github.com/Dronecode/DronecodeSDK/blob/develop/core/system_impl.cpp#L518

Would do that do the job?

but it is not exposed in [system.cpp](https://github.com/Dronecode/DronecodeSDK/blob/develop/core/system.cpp)

@julianoes Of course it is possible. It is not about possibility, it's about convenience.

Assume there is a set of 50 drones (maybe more!), which are labeled from 1 to 50. How you would you tell the user that drone 19 and 47 have some issues and require some checking?

I guess it's harder for the user to look for drones that have UUID 5345213345 and 6968987123 than just 19 and 47, no?

issue.body:
I don't understand UUID is  became AUTOPILOT_VERSION's uid.

To explain my situation I have five drones.
According to the example, when dronecore make a system that need UUID. 

like below
DroneCore dc;
//... 
//Use UUID named uuid to get the associated System
System &system = dc.system(uuid);

But in dronecore's UUID is declared AUTOPILOT_VERSION's uid. My five drones have same AUTOPILOT_VERSION's uid and have different system ID. So, my five drones collision each others. 
I have question, my AUTOPILOT_VERSION's uid are same is strange or dronecore's uuid is strange?

I would be grateful if you could help with this phenomenon.

